{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 34, :comments ["<b>Or has any trial</b> In this verse is the entire alphabet. The Holy One, blessed be He, wished to give them the Torah in Egypt, but the Mem-Heh-Dalet said: test them first. Therefore הנסה appears twice in the Masorah: here and the other הנסה דבר אליך תלאה, where the Mem-Heh-Dalet said: test them before You make Your words heard to them. The verse begins with alef and ends with kaf — אך טוב לישראל, and it equals numerically the name אהיה, by which He revealed Himself to Moses to bring them out of Egypt, and by which He smites the idolaters, as it is written: ואך אותך. Seven things are mentioned here in the trials: במסות, באותות, במופתים, במלחמה, ביד חזקה, בזרוע נטויה, ובמוראים גדולים — corresponding to the seven days that each plague lasted, and corresponding to the seven days of Passover." "<b>in Egypt before your eyes</b> And adjacent to it: אתה הראת — when You executed judgments in Egypt, and through their gods You made them know that You are YHWH."], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :baal-haturim, :lang :en, :chapter 4, :notes {0 "The phrase מה\"ד (Mem-Heh-Dalet) is left as acronym; in context it appears to be a personified entity that speaks, but its expansion is uncertain and left untranslated. The phrase ואך אותך is left unaddressed as Ba'al HaTurim cites it without a verse reference. The phrase אך טוב לישראל appears to be a mnemonic linking the verse's alef-to-kaf span to Psalm 73:1; its logical connection is preserved as written."}, :source-witnesses [["On Your Way" "http://mobile.tora.ws/"]], :timestamp "2026-07-12T19:08:52.732970811Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}